{
  "requirement": "So that concurrent assessments find their system prompt cached under\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.cache_checkpoints`, a batch MUST start one\nassessment of each kind it contains before any other of that kind, and start the\nothers once its first conversation with a provider attempt finishes, including a failed conversation.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests cover both obligations of `requirements.authoring.cache_warmup`, including the failed-conversation case the text names.\n\n**Obligation 1: start one assessment of each kind before any other of that kind.**\n- The unit test `src/authoring/batch.rs#mod[tests]/fn[each_kind_starts_one_assessment_until_its_first_conversation_finishes]` checks that `CacheWarmup::next` starts one `requirement` and one `example`, flagged `warms=true`. It checks that the next call returns `None` while both kinds are warming.\n- It also checks that an assessment that finished without any request (`finished`) lets the next of its kind warm instead.\n- `src/authoring/batch.rs#mod[tests]/fn[structural_and_test_reviews_warm_separately_without_changing_usage_labels]` checks that structural and behavioural requirements are separate kinds. Each kind gets its own warming assessment, and the second one in each kind waits.\n- `tests/authoring_batch.rs#fn[a_batch_warms_each_kind_with_one_assessment_before_starting_the_others]` runs a real `author --all` against a mock provider. It uses the helper `assert_warmed_first`, which checks that the first request of each kind arrives before any other of that kind. It asserts 4 requirement requests and 2 example requests.\n\n**Obligation 2: start the others once the first conversation finishes, including a failed one.**\n- The unit tests check that `warmed(kind)` releases the waiting subjects with `warms=false`. They also check that a kind stays blocked until its own `warmed`, and that a different kind's `warmed` does not release it.\n- `assert_warmed_first` checks that every other subject of the kind arrived at or after the warming request was answered. The test above also asserts that the post-warmup requests run concurrently (`peak >= 3`), so the others do start once released.\n- A failed first conversation:\n  - `tests/authoring_batch.rs#fn[failed_assessment_keeps_other_results_and_reports_failure]` has the first requirement (`alpha`) fail with a provider error. It asserts that the other results are still produced, and that peers started only after the failure was answered. It also asserts the usage record shows `failed_attempts == 1`.\n  - `tests/authoring_batch.rs#fn[a_failed_first_request_still_releases_its_kind]` throttles the first request once, then retries it. It asserts that peers arrive only after the retry was answered, and that all the others are released and complete.\n\n**Gaps considered.** I found no gap that needs a fix.\n- Every request in the warmup fixtures is a single-request conversation. Tool-call turns within the first conversation are not tied to the release point. The throttle-and-retry test already shows the release waits for the whole conversation, not just the first request. The text's boundaries (one per kind, release on finish, failure also releases, kinds are independent) are all asserted.\n- I read the implementation (`CacheWarmup`, `run_batch`, `UsageLog::observe_usage`) only to understand what the helpers exercise. I derived the expectations from the requirement text.",
  "references": "c5c9d2da8548a8856dd3dfa7718c8ed52af57203e491cc5213606d5c78db9450",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.authoring.cache_warmup",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.authoring.cache_warmup"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} cache_warmup",
        "So that concurrent assessments find their system prompt cached under",
        "{cite}`requirements.authoring.cache_checkpoints`, a batch MUST start one",
        "assessment of each kind it contains before any other of that kind, and start the",
        "others once its first conversation with a provider attempt finishes, including a failed conversation.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.authoring.cache_checkpoints",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.authoring.cache_checkpoints"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} cache_checkpoints",
        "For backends that accept explicit cache checkpoints, when the backend and model",
        "support prompt caching, every provider request MUST place one cache checkpoint",
        "after the system prompt of {cite}`requirements.authoring.prompt_parts` and",
        "another at the end of its last message, so each request reuses the tool",
        "definitions and system prompt that earlier assessments of its kind sent, and",
        "the conversation its own earlier requests sent. Cache checkpoints MUST NOT",
        "change the content of a request.",
        "",
        "For OpenAI, which caches matching prompt prefixes automatically, the harness",
        "MUST keep the system prompt and tool definitions unchanged across requests in",
        "one conversation and replay prior conversation content in its original order.",
        "It MUST NOT insert checkpoint blocks.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
